# PIP-MON .gitignore
#
# Written BEFORE the first commit, deliberately. The rule this encodes:
#   a build gate that regenerates a derived artefact must never be able to
#   commit that artefact by accident, because then a stale copy can be
#   reviewed and merged as though it were source.
#
# Everything excluded here is either (a) regenerated by build_all.bat, or
# (b) vendored from elsewhere and already has its own upstream.

# --- build outputs ---------------------------------------------------------
build/
vprobe/build/
launcher/build/
overseer_cli/build/
dist/
*.obj
*.pdb
*.ilk
*.exp
*.lib
*.exe
*.dll

# --- staging: a deployment staging area, not source -----------------------
# Holds copies of the runtime and the database laid out as they are installed.
stage/

# --- generated database artefacts ------------------------------------------
# vprobe/libs/fo4_*.addresses.txt is the PER-BUILD SOURCE and IS tracked: it is
# the database every build derives from and byte-compares against.
#
# The .bin next to it is NOT tracked, for two reasons. It is derived, and
# vprobe/build_libbin.bat regenerates and byte-compares it on every build, so
# committing it would let a stale copy be reviewed as though it were source.
# And it is MACHINE-SPECIFIC: its header carries an FNV-1a hash of the local
# Fallout4.exe's PE headers, so one committed copy would be wrong for every
# other install. Each machine generates its own from the same .txt.
vprobe/libs/*.addresses.bin
vprobe/libs/deploy/

# --- generated Papyrus bytecode --------------------------------------------
# Produced from PipMon.psc by build_papyrus.bat, which verifies the PEX magic.
# Tracked source is the .psc, not the compiled artefact.
*.pex
*.asm

# --- vendored third-party --------------------------------------------------
# third_party/CommonLibF4 is a full .git clone of a third-party repository
# (19.5 MB). It carries its own history and must never be flattened into ours.
# Our use of it is documented, and it is data-only: nothing links against it.
third_party/

# --- python ----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# --- rust ------------------------------------------------------------------
target/
**/*.rs.bk

# --- editor / OS -----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# --- logs and crash dumps --------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.dmp
crash.log
Papyrus.0.log

# --- generated retrieval index ----------------------------------------------
# rag/index.sqlite3 is DERIVED, and it is derived by a build stage, so the rule
# at the top of this file applies exactly as it does to the address blob: a
# stale copy committed as though it were source is a second, silently diverging
# copy of the truth. tools/rag_index.py rebuilds it, and stage 6 of build_all.bat
# reindexes before it self-tests, so nothing is lost by excluding it.
#
# The CONFIGURATION beside it is tracked, and deliberately so: ai_context/
# vocabulary.json and ranking.json are the vocabulary and the weight model, they
# are hand-authored, and a review of a weight change is a review of a ranking
# change. Those are source.
rag/index.sqlite3
rag/index.sqlite3-journal
rag/index.sqlite3-wal
rag/index.sqlite3-shm

# --- local scratch ---------------------------------------------------------
*.bak
*.tmp

# --- test environment run directories --------------------------------------
# Written by tools/testenv.ps1. One per live session, and each one carries a
# full copy of vprobe.dll plus BOTH forms of the address database.
#
# This is untracked for the same reason stage/ and third_party/ are, and the
# reason is the .gitignore rule itself: a derived artefact that a build or a
# test run can produce must never be committable, because then a stale copy
# gets reviewed and merged as though it were source. A committed run
# directory would be a second, silently diverging copy of the runtime and of
# the address database -- the exact class of bug the stale-blob guard in
# build_all.bat exists to catch.
runs/
# Session backups. These are snapshots taken while repairing a file in place.
# The originals are all in git history; keeping five near-identical copies of
# gen_facility.pas in the tree is noise, and one of them (bak-premerge) is the
# 290-line rewrite whose diff is the thing worth remembering, not the file.
*.bak-*
*.bak
tools/*.bak-*
testfacility/**/*.bak-*
